                <h2>观点：同质化不是 AI 的问题，而是企业把 AI 当成了模板机器</h2>
<p>AI 广告文案最容易陷入的误区，是把“生成”直接等同于“创意”。输入产品名称、核心卖点和目标受众后，模型很快能给出标题、描述与行动号召。但不同品牌使用相似模板、相似词汇，最终投放素材就像同一批复制品。</p>
<p>真正的问题不在于文案由谁生成，而在于品牌有没有给模型足够的独特信息。以渔在服务海外广告账户开户与投放客户时发现，团队往往拥有真实素材，却没有将其整理成模型可用的输入：客服记录里的高频疑问、销售团队使用过的表达、用户评论中的关键阻力，以及竞品广告中尚未被解决的问题。</p>
<p>如果这些信息没有进入生成环节，AI 只能依赖公开内容进行安全而通用的组合。结论很明确：避免 AI 文案同质化，不能只靠调整提示词，而要建立一条从业务事实到广告表达的内容链。</p>
<h2>先补齐四类“独家输入”，再让 AI 动笔</h2>
<h3>1. 场景，而不是宽泛人群</h3>
<p>不要只告诉模型受众是“25—35 岁职场人群”。更有用的输入是：他们何时产生需求，正在比较什么，为什么迟迟不点击。一个工具类 App 可以提供“用户在周末规划旅行时需要快速核算预算”等具体场景；某跨境电商团队则可以输入“买家发现运费高于商品价格后产生弃单”的真实阻力。</p>
<p>场景越具体，AI 越容易生成有针对性的文案，而不是泛泛强调便利、节省时间和品质。</p>
<h3>2. 产品证据，而不是形容词</h3>
<p>“高效、智能、值得信赖”几乎不能形成记忆点。应替换为可验证的信息，例如是否支持多币种账户、能否批量查看余额、充值后到账需要多久、告警阈值是否可自定义。涉及速度、费用或效果时，还要注明适用条件，避免生成无法证明的绝对化承诺。</p>
<h3>3. 客户原话，而不是品牌自述</h3>
<p>客服工单、评论区、搜索词和销售录音，是降低 AI 腔调最有效的素材库。可先人工整理高频表达，再让 AI 改写，而不是把整套品牌手册交给模型后要求“写得有创意”。原始语言通常比模型自行想象的用户语言更可信。</p>
<h3>4. 明确的反面清单</h3>
<p>在提示词中直接列出禁止使用的词、不可出现的承诺和需要避开的市场表达。例如要求不使用“最领先”“零风险”“永久免费”，并注明不得暗示未经平台确认的充值到账速度。限制不是削弱创意，而是在为合规和品牌一致性设置边界。</p>
<h2>一套可执行的“生成—筛选—测试”流程</h2>
<p><strong>第一步：生成多个不同角度，而非同义的十个版本。</strong></p>
<p>可以将任务拆分为痛点、对比、操作提示、风险提醒和用户场景。每个角度只输出一版，避免用相近句式制造虚假多样性。</p>
<p><strong>第二步：用五项标准做人工筛选。</strong></p>
<p>查看文案是否包含独有事实、是否符合真实产品能力、是否适合具体渠道、是否容易理解，以及是否与竞品表达明显雷同。任何一项不通过，都应返回输入资料修正，而不是仅靠反复点击“重新生成”。</p>
<p><strong>第三步：把点击率当作文案线索，而非唯一答案。</strong></p>
<p>广告数据只能告诉你哪句话更有效，不能自动解释原因。测试时应同步观察落地页承接、注册或咨询转化，以及不同国家、语言和设备版本的反馈。若点击率上升但转化下降，可能是文案承诺与页面内容不一致。</p>
<h2>运营人员应保留最终判断权</h2>
<p>AI 适合用于整理评论、扩展场景和生成初稿，但不适合单独决定品牌如何表达。尤其在海外广告投放中，涉及开户、充值、账户余额和投放政策的文案，需要核对地区差异、平台规则与实际服务能力。</p>
<p>一个更实用的原则是：模型负责扩大表达选项，运营人员负责选择真实、清晰且可证明的那一句。真正拉开差距的从来不是生成速度，而是企业愿意把哪些独家业务事实交给 AI。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Perspective: Generic Copy Is a Process Problem, Not an AI Problem</h2>
<p>A common mistake is to equate AI generation with creativity. Give the model a product name, a few selling points, and a target audience, and it can quickly produce headlines, descriptions, and calls to action. Yet when brands rely on similar templates and vocabulary, the result is a stream of interchangeable ads.</p>
<p>The issue is not who writes the copy. It is whether the business gives the model distinctive information. From our experience supporting clients with overseas ad account setup and campaign operations, valuable insights often already exist in customer support tickets, sales conversations, product reviews, and repeated objections from prospects. However, those inputs are rarely structured for AI-assisted writing.</p>
<p>If they are not included, the model can only combine safe, generic language from public content. The conclusion is straightforward: differentiated AI copy requires a repeatable chain connecting real business evidence to advertising messages.</p>
<h2>Add Four Types of Exclusive Input Before Writing</h2>
<h3>1. Use situations, not broad audience labels</h3>
<p>“Professionals aged 25 to 35” is too vague. A more useful input explains when the need appears, what alternatives the user compares, and what prevents action. A travel budgeting app might focus on users calculating trip expenses at the weekend, while a cross-border retailer might address shoppers who abandon a purchase after discovering that shipping costs more than the item.</p>
<p>Specific situations help AI produce relevant messages instead of generic claims about convenience or savings.</p>
<h3>2. Provide product evidence, not adjectives</h3>
<p>Terms such as “fast,” “intelligent,” and “trusted” are difficult to remember. Replace them with verifiable details: supported account currencies, bulk balance visibility, recharge processing conditions, or whether alert thresholds can be customized. Any claim about speed, cost, or results should include the conditions under which it applies.</p>
<h3>3. Use customer language, not polished brand claims</h3>
<p>Support tickets, reviews, search queries, and sales recordings are among the best sources for reducing generic AI tone. Let AI rewrite the language after a person has identified the recurring questions and concerns. Actual customer expressions are often more credible than personas invented by the model.</p>
<h3>4. Create an explicit exclusion list</h3>
<p>Tell the model which words, promises, and market-specific expressions to avoid. This may include unsupported claims such as “risk-free,” “permanently free,” or “fastest recharge,” along with any language that conflicts with regional requirements or actual service capabilities. These boundaries protect compliance and brand consistency.</p>
<h2>A Practical Generate–Filter–Test Workflow</h2>
<p><strong>First, generate different angles rather than ten synonymous versions.</strong></p>
<p>Separate the task into pain points, comparisons, instructions, risk reminders, and use cases. Produce one draft per angle so that diversity comes from strategy, not random wording.</p>
<p><strong>Second, apply a five-point human review.</strong></p>
<p>Check whether the copy contains a distinctive fact, matches actual product capabilities, suits the chosen channel, is immediately understandable, and differs clearly from competitor language. If it fails, fix the source inputs rather than repeatedly asking the model to regenerate the same brief.</p>
<p><strong>Third, treat click-through rate as a clue rather than the final verdict.</strong></p>
<p>Performance data indicates which message attracts attention, but it does not always explain why. Review landing-page consistency, registration or inquiry conversion, and results by country, language, and device. A higher click-through rate paired with lower conversion may indicate a mismatch between the ad promise and the page experience.</p>
<h2>Keep Human Judgment in the Final Decision</h2>
<p>AI is well suited to organizing reviews, expanding use cases, and drafting initial options. It should not independently determine how a brand communicates. In overseas advertising, copy involving account setup, recharge, balances, or campaign policies must be checked against regional differences, platform rules, and actual service capabilities.</p>
<p>The operating principle is simple: let the model expand the range of expressions, while the team selects the one that is truthful, clear, and provable. Speed is rarely the main advantage. The real advantage comes from deciding which exclusive business evidence enters the AI workflow.</p>
